  "text": "In May 2022, Acme Corp, customer forty-two, placed a twelve hundred dollar order from the East. The next summer, it moved West. Today, which region does that order belong to? Both answers can be right: the East, as it was, or the West, as it is. What the warehouse says depends on how the customer's row took the move. That row is a slowly changing dimension, or SCD. Nine questions on dimensions that change, and on data that arrives late: seven worked through, then two quick ones.\n\nQuestion one. What is SCD Type one, and when should you use it? Type one overwrites. Acme's region becomes West, there's still one row, and no trace of the East. So the past moves with it. The 2022 order now reports as West. Overwrite every customer that way, and every region's 2022 revenue changes. East loses twelve hundred dollars, in a report that already went out. Use it when the old value was never true, like a typo in a name, or when the business only ever asks about now. In practice it's a merge on the natural key: update the row if it's there, insert it if not. And types are chosen per column. A dimension that versions the region can still treat the name as Type one, so a fixed misspelling is fixed on every version.",
